= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Since the real <conformal factor> $\Lambda$ is nowhere zero, $\Lambda^2>0$ at every point. For any <tangent vector>,
$$
\widetilde g(V,V)=\Lambda^2g(V,V).
$$
The sign is unchanged. With <metric signature> $(-,+,+,+)$, negative, zero and positive values therefore describe the same <timelike vectors>, <null vectors> and <spacelike vectors> for both <metric tensors>. In particular the two <null cones> coincide.

Choose the same <time orientation> for the two <Lorentzian metrics>. A future-directed <causal curve> or <timelike curve> for one has the same character for the other. Hence the <causal future>, <causal past> and <chronological future> of every point agree:
$$
\boxed{
J^\pm_{\widetilde g}(p)=J^\pm_g(p),\qquad
I^\pm_{\widetilde g}(p)=I^\pm_g(p).
}
$$
This is the preservation of <causal structure> by a smooth, nonvanishing <conformal transformation>. It does not assert preservation of <proper time> or of <affine parameter> lengths.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Put $\omega=\log|\Lambda|$, which is smooth because $\Lambda$ is nowhere zero. The <conformal change of the Levi-Civita connection> is
$$
\widetilde\Gamma^a{}_{bc}-\Gamma^a{}_{bc}
=C^a{}_{bc}
=\delta^a_b\partial_c\omega+\delta^a_c\partial_b\omega
-g_{bc}g^{ad}\partial_d\omega.
$$
This follows directly by differentiating $\widetilde g_{ab}=e^{2\omega}g_{ab}$ and using $\widetilde g^{ab}=e^{-2\omega}g^{ab}$ in the <Christoffel symbol> formula.

Let $V=dx/d\tau$ be a <geodesic> tangent for $g$, with $\tau$ an <affine parameter>. Since $\nabla_VV=0$,
$$
\boxed{
\widetilde\nabla_VV
=2V(\omega)V-g(V,V)\operatorname{grad}_g\omega.
}
$$
For a <null geodesic> the second term vanishes. The remaining acceleration is tangent to the same curve and can be removed by changing its parameter. Explicitly set $\widetilde V=fV$, with $f=d\tau/d\widetilde\tau$. Then
$$
\widetilde\nabla_{\widetilde V}\widetilde V
=f^2\left(2\frac{d\omega}{d\tau}+\frac{d\log f}{d\tau}\right)V.
$$
It vanishes when $f=C^{-1}\Lambda^{-2}$, or
$$
\boxed{\frac{d\widetilde\tau}{d\tau}=C\Lambda^2,\qquad C>0.}
$$
This <affine parameter change under conformal rescaling> shows that <conformal preservation of null geodesic paths> means preservation of unparametrized curves; the original <affine parameter> usually changes.

For a non-null <geodesic>, the component of $\operatorname{grad}_g\omega$ orthogonal to $V$ generally survives. A change of parameter can remove only the tangential component of acceleration. As a concrete example use <Minkowski spacetime>,
$$
g=-dt^2+dx^2+dy^2+dz^2,\qquad
\Lambda=e^{kx},\quad k\ne0.
$$
The curve $(t,x,y,z)=(\tau,0,0,0)$ is a <timelike geodesic> for $g$, but along it
$$
\widetilde\nabla_{\partial_t}\partial_t=k\partial_x.
$$
This acceleration is not parallel to $\partial_t$, so the curve is not a <geodesic> of $\widetilde g$ under any reparametrization. Special non-null curves may still be preserved, for example when $\Lambda$ is constant; nullness is the general guarantee.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The product rule for the <Lie derivative> and the <Killing equation> give
$$
\mathcal L_K\widetilde g
=\mathcal L_K(\Lambda^2g)
=K(\Lambda^2)g+\Lambda^2\mathcal L_Kg
=2\frac{K(\Lambda)}{\Lambda}\widetilde g.
$$
Consequently $K$ is a <conformal Killing vector field> for the rescaled <metric tensor>, with smooth <conformal Killing factor>
$$
\boxed{\sigma=K(\log|\Lambda|)=\frac{K^a\partial_a\Lambda}{\Lambda}.}
$$
It remains an actual <Killing vector field> precisely when $K(\Lambda)=0$: the <conformal factor> must be constant along its <integral curves of a vector field>.

