= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A nondegenerate <Lorentzian metric> requires $M\ne0$; take $M>0$ as the radius convention. The original coordinates describe $\lambda>0$, with $\lambda=0$ a candidate boundary rather than an ordinary point of that chart. The change of coordinate gives
$$
dt=dv+\frac{M}{\lambda^2}d\lambda.
$$
Substituting and cancelling the two terms proportional to $d\lambda^2/\lambda^2$ produces
$$
\boxed{
ds^2=-\lambda^2dv^2-2M\,dv\,d\lambda+M^2d\Omega^2.
}
$$
The $(v,\lambda)$ block of this <metric tensor> has <determinant> $-M^2$ and <inverse metric>
$$
\begin{pmatrix}
g^{vv}&g^{v\lambda}\\
g^{\lambda v}&g^{\lambda\lambda}
\end{pmatrix}
=
\begin{pmatrix}
0&-1/M\\
-1/M&\lambda^2/M^2
\end{pmatrix}.
$$
All coefficients are smooth through $\lambda=0$, and the block has one negative and one positive <eigenvalue>. Together with the positive round <two-sphere> factor this is a regular <Lorentzian metric>. It extends to a neighborhood with either sign of $\lambda$. The usual polar-coordinate problem at the poles of the <two-sphere> is removed by separate angular charts. Therefore $\lambda=0$ is only a <coordinate singularity> of the original chart. This is the degenerate-horizon patch of <Bertotti-Robinson spacetime>.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

On the extension in part (i), the <hypersurface> $\mathcal N$ has nonzero normal <covector> $n_a=\partial_a\lambda$. Its squared <norm> is
$$
g^{ab}n_an_b=g^{\lambda\lambda}=\frac{\lambda^2}{M^2}.
$$
This vanishes on $\mathcal N$, which is therefore a <null hypersurface>. Raising the index gives
$$
n^a=-\frac1M(\partial_v)^a+\frac{\lambda^2}{M^2}(\partial_\lambda)^a.
$$
Since $\partial_t=\partial_v$ at fixed $\lambda$, the original <Killing vector field> becomes the smooth nonzero field $K=\partial_v$. The transformed <metric tensor> is independent of $v$, so $K$ remains a <Killing vector field>, and
$$
\left.n^a\right|_{\mathcal N}=-\frac1M K^a,\qquad
\left.g(K,K)\right|_{\mathcal N}=0.
$$
Thus $K$ is both tangent and normal to $\mathcal N$. This is exactly a <Killing horizon>:
$$
\boxed{\mathcal N=\{\lambda=0\}\text{ is a Killing horizon of }\partial_t=\partial_v.}
$$
The <Killing vector field> itself does not vanish there; a vanishing squared <norm> means nullness, not a zero vector.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The <Killing equation> implies
$$
K^a\nabla_aK^b=-\frac12\nabla^b(K^aK_a).
$$
Here $K^2=-\lambda^2$, so in the regular coordinates
$$
K^a\nabla_aK^b
=\lambda\,g^{b\lambda}
=-\frac{\lambda}{M}(\partial_v)^b
+\frac{\lambda^3}{M^2}(\partial_\lambda)^b.
$$
This vector vanishes at $\lambda=0$. The <Killing vector field> $K=\partial_v$ is nonzero there, so comparison with the definition of <surface gravity> yields
$$
\boxed{\kappa=0.}
$$
Hence this is a <degenerate Killing horizon>. The argument uses the vanishing gradient of $K^2$ on the horizon; the condition $K^2=0$ by itself would not force zero <surface gravity>.

