= a
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A regular <hypersurface> $f=0$, with $df\ne0$, is a <null hypersurface> if its normal <covector> has zero squared <norm>:
$$
g^{ab}(\partial_af)(\partial_bf)=0\quad\text{on }f=0.
$$
Its normal <vector> is then also tangent to the <hypersurface>; the induced <metric tensor> is degenerate.

For the <Kerr metric> the normal to a constant-$r$ surface has squared <norm>
$$
g^{rr}=\frac{\Delta}{\Sigma},\qquad
\Delta=(r-r_+)(r-r_-),\qquad
r_\pm=M\pm\sqrt{M^2-a^2}.
$$
For $0<a<M$ the two roots are positive and distinct. To verify that they are genuine regular <null hypersurfaces>, rather than trusting singular <Boyer-Lindquist coordinates>, introduce <ingoing Kerr coordinates>
$$
dv=dt+\frac{r^2+a^2}{\Delta}\,dr,\qquad
d\psi=d\phi+\frac a\Delta\,dr.
$$
Substitution gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
ds^2={}&-\left(1-\frac{2Mr}{\Sigma}\right)dv^2
+2\,dv\,dr+\Sigma\,d\theta^2
-2a\sin^2\theta\,dr\,d\psi\\
&-\frac{4Mar\sin^2\theta}{\Sigma}\,dv\,d\psi
+\sin^2\theta\left(r^2+a^2+
\frac{2Ma^2r\sin^2\theta}{\Sigma}\right)d\psi^2.
\end{aligned}
$$
There are no $\Delta^{-1}$ terms. At either root $\Sigma>0$, and the <determinant> is $-\Sigma^2\sin^2\theta$ away from the angular coordinate poles. Thus the <metric tensor> extends smoothly across each root. The raised normal on either surface is
$$
\left.(d r)^\sharp\right|_{r_\pm}
=\frac{r_\pm^2+a^2}{\Sigma}
\left(\partial_v+\Omega_\pm\partial_\psi\right),
\qquad
\Omega_\pm=\frac{a}{r_\pm^2+a^2}.
$$
This is a nonzero null <Killing vector field> normal to the surface. Both surfaces are <Killing horizons>, with <horizon angular velocities> $\Omega_\pm$.

**The event-horizon terminology in the paper needs qualification.** In the usual asymptotically flat <Kerr black hole> extension, relative to a chosen exterior's <future null infinity>, the future branch of $r=r_+$ is the <event horizon>, the boundary $\partial J^-(\mathscr I^+)$. In the intervening black-hole region $\Delta<0$, the coordinate $r$ has timelike gradient and future-directed curves move toward smaller $r$; they cannot return across the outer boundary to that exterior. The inner surface $r=r_-$ is a <Cauchy horizon>, not a second <event horizon> for the same asymptotic end. Nullness is a local property; identifying an <event horizon> additionally requires this global <causal structure>.<General Relativity by David Tong>[Sections 6.3.2 and 6.2.4: regular extension across the two Kerr roots, global diagram and the inner Cauchy horizon]{ref}

The allowed endpoint $a=0$ provides an especially concrete counterexample to the unrestricted description of both roots. The <Kerr metric> then becomes <Schwarzschild spacetime>, $r_+=2M$ is its regular <event horizon>, but $r_-=0$ is a <curvature singularity>:
$$
R_{abcd}R^{abcd}=\frac{48M^2}{r^6}\longrightarrow\infty.
$$
It is not a regular inner <null hypersurface>. Thus the two-horizon calculation above assumes $0<a<M$.

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <metric tensor> is independent of $t$ and $\phi$, so
$$
\boxed{T=\partial_t,\qquad \Phi=\partial_\phi}
$$
are commuting <Killing vector fields>. For $0<a<M$ they span the continuous <Killing vector field> algebra; every constant linear combination is also a <Killing vector field>. They express <stationary spacetime> and axial <symmetry>, respectively. At the allowed endpoint $a=0$ the <spherical symmetry> of <Schwarzschild spacetime> enlarges the algebra: in addition to $T$, a basis of rotational <Killing vector fields> is
$$
J_x=-\sin\phi\,\partial_\theta-\cot\theta\cos\phi\,\partial_\phi,
\qquad
J_y=\cos\phi\,\partial_\theta-\cot\theta\sin\phi\,\partial_\phi,
\qquad
J_z=\partial_\phi.
$$
Their polar-coordinate expressions are singular at the axis, but the rotational fields are smooth there.

Let $U^a=dx^a/ds$ be the tangent to any affinely parametrized <geodesic>. For a <Killing vector field> $X$,
$$
\frac{d}{ds}(X_aU^a)
=U^aU^b\nabla_aX_b+X_bU^a\nabla_aU^b
=U^aU^b\nabla_{(a}X_{b)}=0.
$$
This is the <geodesic conserved quantity from a Killing vector>. With $p_a=g_{ab}U^b$, choose the <energy> and axial <angular momentum> conventions
$$
\boxed{E=-p_t=-g(T,U),\qquad L=p_\phi=g(\Phi,U).}
$$
For a massive particle with <proper time> parameter these are per unit rest mass; for a <null geodesic> their common scale depends on the <affine parameter> normalization. Expanding in the supplied <Boyer-Lindquist coordinates> gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\left(1-\frac{2Mr}{\Sigma}\right)\dot t
+\frac{2Mar\sin^2\theta}{\Sigma}\dot\phi,\\
L&=-\frac{2Mar\sin^2\theta}{\Sigma}\dot t
+\frac{\sin^2\theta}{\Sigma}
\left[(r^2+a^2)^2-a^2\Delta\sin^2\theta\right]\dot\phi.
\end{aligned}
$$
The factor of two between $g_{t\phi}$ and the coefficient of $dt\,d\phi$ is essential for these signs and coefficients.

= c
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Reflection across $\theta=\pi/2$ is an <isometry>; a <geodesic> initially tangent to its fixed equatorial plane remains in that plane by uniqueness of the <geodesic equation>. Thus take $\theta=\pi/2$, $\dot\theta=0$, and $\Sigma=r^2$. Define the algebraic abbreviations
$$
A=1-\frac{2M}{r},\qquad
B=\frac{2Ma}{r},\qquad
C=r^2+a^2+\frac{2Ma^2}{r}.
$$
The <conserved quantities> of part (b) become
$$
E=A\dot t+B\dot\phi,\qquad
L=-B\dot t+C\dot\phi,
\qquad
AC+B^2=\Delta.
$$
For $\Delta\ne0$, inversion gives
$$
\dot t=\frac{CE-BL}{\Delta},\qquad
\dot\phi=\frac{BE+AL}{\Delta}.
$$
The <null condition> can be written without expanding a quadratic square:
$$
0=-E\dot t+L\dot\phi+\frac{r^2}{\Delta}\dot r^2.
$$
Substituting the preceding expressions yields the <equatorial Kerr null geodesic radial equation>
$$
\boxed{
\dot r^2
=E^2+\frac{a^2E^2-L^2}{r^2}
+\frac{2M(L-aE)^2}{r^3}.
}
$$
An equivalent useful polynomial form is
$$
\boxed{
r^4\dot r^2=\mathcal R(r),\qquad
\mathcal R(r)=\left[E(r^2+a^2)-aL\right]^2
-\Delta(L-aE)^2.
}
$$
These expressions use an <affine parameter> $s$; a common rescaling of $E$ and $L$ accompanies its rescaling. The inversion was performed in the exterior $r>r_+$, where <Boyer-Lindquist coordinates> are regular. The polynomial radial identity has a smooth continuation to a horizon, but the formulas for $\dot t$ and $\dot\phi$ there must be replaced by regular-coordinate expressions. In the limit $a=0$, the result reduces to the <Schwarzschild spacetime> equation $\dot r^2=E^2-(1-2M/r)L^2/r^2$.

= d
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

**The assertion about every exterior radius is false for geodesics.** A <circular orbit> here means an equatorial <null geodesic> at constant $r$, rather than a genuinely radial trajectory. A zero of the radial <effective potential> alone gives a turning point, not necessarily a <circular orbit>. The radial <geodesic equation> must hold as well.

Write $q=L-aE$ and
$$
f(r)=\dot r^2=E^2-\frac{q^2+2aEq}{r^2}+\frac{2Mq^2}{r^3}.
$$
For an exterior <circular null geodesic>, the two conditions are $f(r)=f'(r)=0$, equivalently $\mathcal R(r)=\mathcal R'(r)=0$ with $E,L$ fixed. One way to justify the derivative condition without dividing by $\dot r=0$ is to use the radial <Euler-Lagrange equation>. At constant $r$ it gives
$$
\partial_rg_{AB}\dot x^A\dot x^B=0,\qquad A,B\in\{t,\phi\}.
$$
Together with $g_{AB}\dot x^A\dot x^B=0$, and the fixed conserved $p_A$, this is precisely the double-root condition for $\mathcal R$.

Now
$$
f'(r)=\frac{2(q^2+2aEq)}{r^3}-\frac{6Mq^2}{r^4}.
$$
Setting this to zero and using $f=0$ gives
$$
q^2+2aEq=\frac{3Mq^2}{r},\qquad
E^2=\frac{Mq^2}{r^3}.
$$
A nontrivial exterior <null geodesic> cannot have $E=0$ here: the second equation would force $q=L=0$, and then every tangent component would vanish. Therefore
$$
\boxed{
r^2=\frac{L^2-a^2E^2}{3E^2},\qquad
r^3=\frac{M(L-aE)^2}{E^2}.
}
$$
Both equations, with the same constants $E,L$, are required. They give the requested radius relations in terms of the <conserved quantities>, while showing why an arbitrary exterior radius is not permitted.

For a more explicit classification let $b=L/E$ be the <impact parameter>. The second relation gives $b-a=s\,r^{3/2}/\sqrt M$ with $s=\pm1$. Substituting into the first relation yields
$$
\boxed{r^2-3Mr+2s\,a\sqrt{Mr}=0.}
$$
For $0<a<M$, $s=+1$ gives the prograde <equatorial circular Kerr photon orbit> and $s=-1$ the retrograde one. The two allowed exterior radii are
$$
\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
r_{\mathrm{pro}}
&=2M\left[1+\cos\left(\frac23\arccos(-a/M)\right)\right],\\
r_{\mathrm{retro}}
&=2M\left[1+\cos\left(\frac23\arccos(a/M)\right)\right].
\end{aligned}
}
$$
Both are strictly greater than $r_+$ in the subextremal range. Squaring the signed radius equation gives $r(r-3M)^2=4Ma^2$, but introduces an additional inner root and loses the sign distinguishing the two senses of rotation. At $a=0$ the two exterior radii coincide at the <photon sphere> $r=3M$, with $b=\pm3\sqrt3M$.

\Image[2017-kerr-photon-orbit-radii.png]
{title=The two equatorial circular photon-orbit radii and the outer Kerr horizon as functions of spin}

For a direct counterexample to the all-radii claim take $a=0$ and $r_0=4M>r_+=2M$. Choose $E=1$ and $L=4\sqrt2M$ so that $f(r_0)=0$. The radial <geodesic equation> nevertheless gives
$$
\ddot r=\frac12f'(r)
=\frac{L^2(r-3M)}{r^4},
\qquad
\left.\ddot r\right|_{r=4M}=\frac1{8M}\ne0.
$$
It is a turning point, not a <circular orbit>. Alternatively, constant-$r$ null directions exist throughout the exterior because the two angular-velocity roots of $g_{tt}+2\Omega g_{t\phi}+\Omega^2g_{\phi\phi}=0$ are real; almost all of their integral curves require acceleration and fail the radial <geodesic equation>. This distinction explains how an all-radii assertion can arise.

Finally, $r=r_+$ itself has the <null geodesic> generators of its <Killing horizon>, proportional to $\chi_+=\partial_v+\Omega_+\partial_\psi$. They are a separate horizon case. On the horizon $\chi_+$ is normal to every tangent, so its contractions with $\partial_v$ and $\partial_\psi$ both vanish: $E=L=0$ for a generator tangent. Its <affine parameter> can be chosen by rescaling that tangent. The exterior calculation dividing by $E$ does not include it. The presence of these generators does not supply <circular null geodesics> at the intervening arbitrary exterior radii.
